import{rt as e,t,tt as n,xt as r}from"./chunks/framework.CuR1irRm.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Casbin Remembers, the Database Changes","description":"","frontmatter":{"authorship":["ai"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"stories/casbin-policy-not-watching-db.md","filePath":"stories/casbin-policy-not-watching-db.md"}`),a={name:`stories/casbin-policy-not-watching-db.md`};function o(t,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),n(`div`,{"data-pagefind-body":!0,"data-pagefind-meta":`date:1794111317000`},[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="casbin-remembers-the-database-changes" tabindex="-1">Casbin Remembers, the Database Changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#casbin-remembers-the-database-changes" aria-label="Permalink to “Casbin Remembers, the Database Changes”">​</a></h1><p><em>Why a casbin enforcer doesn&#39;t see policy rows written by anyone else, and what that means for seeding and for running more than one authorization instance.</em></p><p>Written with AI during billing-03, October 2026. Not fixed yet; this is the problem statement for a later phase.</p><hr><h2 id="how-the-enforcer-holds-policy" tabindex="-1">How the Enforcer Holds Policy <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-enforcer-holds-policy" aria-label="Permalink to “How the Enforcer Holds Policy”">​</a></h2><p>The authorization service checks permissions with a casbin <code>TransactionalEnforcer</code> backed by Postgres (gorm adapter). At startup it calls <code>LoadPolicy()</code> once: every row of <code>casbin_rule</code> is read into memory, and the <code>g</code> rows are turned into the in-memory role graph (see <a href="./casbin-role-manager-clobber.html">the role manager story</a>).</p><p>After that, <strong>every <code>Enforce</code> call reads memory only</strong>. The database is touched only when <em>this</em> enforcer writes (<code>AddPolicy</code>, <code>RemoveGroupingPolicy</code>, …): it updates its memory and saves the row, through autosave.</p><div class="language-" dir="ltr"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes catppuccin-latte catppuccin-mocha" style="--shiki-light:#4c4f69;--shiki-dark:#cdd6f4;--shiki-light-bg:#eff1f5;--shiki-dark-bg:#1e1e2e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>process A: memory ◄── LoadPolicy() at start ── casbin_rule ──► LoadPolicy() at start ──► memory :process B</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>           A writes → A memory + DB                              B never hears about it</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Casbin doesn&#39;t poll or listen to the database. A row written by someone else stays invisible until the process reloads.</p><hr><h2 id="where-it-bites" tabindex="-1">Where It Bites <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-it-bites" aria-label="Permalink to “Where It Bites”">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-a-separate-seed-process" tabindex="-1">1. A separate seed process <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-a-separate-seed-process" aria-label="Permalink to “1. A separate seed process”">​</a></h3><p>If <code>policy.csv</code> sync moves out of the service into <code>cmd/authorizationseed</code> (planned), the seed writes <code>p</code> rows straight into the database. A running authorization instance keeps answering from its old matrix until it restarts. A new permission (say <code>billing_manage</code>) is <strong>denied</strong> by already-running pods even though the row exists.</p><p>Workable rule for now: <strong>seed first, then start or restart authorization.</strong> In Kubernetes that&#39;s a seed Job before the rollout, and a pod that finds the matrix missing reports &quot;not ready&quot; through its startup probe so it gets restarted, instead of serving wrong answers.</p><h3 id="_2-more-than-one-authorization-instance" tabindex="-1">2. More than one authorization instance <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-more-than-one-authorization-instance" aria-label="Permalink to “2. More than one authorization instance”">​</a></h3><p>This one already exists in principle. Instance A handles <code>UpdateWorkspaceMembers</code> and writes the new <code>g</code> row; instance B still has the old role graph. A user just added to a workspace gets <code>403</code> whenever their request lands on B, until B restarts. Removing a member is worse: B keeps <strong>allowing</strong> them.</p><p>Today we run one instance, so nobody has seen it. Scaling authorization horizontally without fixing this would be a security bug.</p><hr><h2 id="ways-out" tabindex="-1">Ways Out <a class="header-anchor" href="#ways-out" aria-label="Permalink to “Ways Out”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>How</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Single instance</td><td>Keep <code>replicas: 1</code></td><td>No HA; the restart rule still applies to seeding</td></tr><tr><td>Periodic reload</td><td><code>e.LoadPolicy()</code> every N seconds</td><td>Simple, but stale for up to N seconds, and a full reload each time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Watcher (Redis)</strong></td><td><a href="https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/casbin/redis-watcher/v2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>casbin/redis-watcher/v2</code></a>: <code>e.SetWatcher(w)</code>; every write publishes on a Redis channel, and other instances run their update callback (classically <code>LoadPolicy()</code>)</td><td>Needs Redis (authorization has none today); near-instant</td></tr><tr><td>Watcher (Postgres)</td><td><a href="https://beta.pkg.go.dev/github.com/IguteChung/casbin-psql-watcher" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>IguteChung/casbin-psql-watcher</code></a> over <code>LISTEN/NOTIFY</code>, also implements incremental <code>WatcherEx</code></td><td>No new infrastructure; community-maintained, check casbin v3 compatibility</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A watcher only fires for writes that go <strong>through an enforcer with the watcher set</strong>. A seed binary would have to set the same watcher, or the running instances would still need a restart after seeding.</p><hr><h2 id="takeaways" tabindex="-1">Takeaways <a class="header-anchor" href="#takeaways" aria-label="Permalink to “Takeaways”">​</a></h2><ul><li>A casbin enforcer is a cache of the policy table with no invalidation by default.</li><li>&quot;Row exists in the database&quot; ≠ &quot;permission granted&quot;: check <code>Enforce</code> on the instance that serves traffic.</li><li>Before running more than one authorization instance, add a watcher (Redis or Postgres <code>LISTEN/NOTIFY</code>), and make the seed binary notify it too.</li></ul>`,24)]])}var s=t(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};